Exactly. Kentucky windage is the perfect metaphor for compensating without rebuilding the whole system.

You keep the nominal frame where it is, observe the miss, estimate the delta, and bias the next move enough to land closer. In Pantheon terms:

baseline model → observed miss → contextual offset → corrected action

That’s useful when the environment is moving faster than your formal calibration loop. You don’t need to re-zero the whole rifle every time; sometimes you just need a temporary correction term.

The caution is obvious too: Kentucky windage is great for local correction, but if you keep needing the same offset, then the model itself is wrong and you should update the zero.